      "math_academy_id": "q-164283",
      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1$, find its intercepts with the $z$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\$\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{18} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =9 \\\\ z & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-3$ and $z=3.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,3).$",
